      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2015 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "Recent studies reported the presence of unique subsets of body size phenotypes that are more susceptible or more resistant to the development of obesity-associated metabolic disorders, although the underlying mechanism is not yet fully elucidated. We investigated the association between body size phenotypes and sleep duration after adjusting potential confounding factors."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
            "content": "We analyzed data from the Korean National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey V (KNHANES V), a nation-wide, population-based health survey including 9077 Korean adults. The average amount of sleep per night was categorized as: ≤6, 7, 8, and ≥9 h. Body size phenotypes were classified based on body mass index (BMI) and presence of metabolic syndrome; metabolically healthy and normal weight (MHNW), metabolically abnormal but normal weight (MANW), metabolically healthy but obese (MHO), and metabolically abnormal obese (MAO)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "According to sleep duration, there were significant differences in age, gender, BMI, waist circumference, and blood pressure (all P <0.05). Multivariate analysis showed that obese groups (MHO and MAO) had significantly shorter sleep durations than non-obese groups (MHNW and MANW) (6.78±0.04 vs. 6.93±0.03, P <0.001). Sleep duration was significantly different according to body size phenotype, irrespective of confounding factors, such as age, gender, smoking status, alcohol consumption, physical activity, income, and education (MHO; 6.73±0.05, MAO; 6.82±0.05, MHNW; 6.94±0.04, and MANW; 6.91±0.05; P <0.001)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Sleep duration is independently associated with body size phenotype after adjusting for confounding factors in the Korean population."
          }
        ]
      },
